// Crewaid — Prisma schema.
//
// ⚠️ TRANSCRIBED VERBATIM FROM PRD §3. FIELD NAMES ARE CONTRACTUAL.
//
// Five other people are coding against these names. Do not rename, do not
// "improve", do not add convenience fields. If a column is genuinely missing,
// revise the PRD first (§0 rule 2: ask rather than guess).
//
// The ONLY deviations from §3, declared explicitly rather than buried:
//
//   1. §3 gives `McpServer.userId String` with no matching relation, and `User`
//      with no `mcpServers` back-reference. Postgres will happily hold that as
//      a bare column, but then deleting a User orphans its McpServer rows and
//      their encrypted credentials — which §7.3 says must not outlive their
//      owner. Added: `McpServer.user` + `User.mcpServers`, onDelete: Cascade.
//
//   2. `Bot.autoReviewRules Json @default("[]")`. Auto-review rules are stored
//      as a Json column on Bot (see packages/shared/src/agent.ts) and validated by
//      `AutoReviewRulesSchema`. Without this column the shared contract claims
//      a field the database does not have.
//
//   3. `Sandbox.lastActiveAt` and `Sandbox.keepRunningUntil`, both nullable.
//      §5 requires idle stop and D-031 requires a keep-running lease, but §3's
//      Sandbox carries no clock for either. Ratified as D-039 §10; the full
//      reasoning is docs/WORKSPACE.md §10.
//
//   4. `Recording.warning RecordingWarning?`. §6/M3 requires that breaching a
//      hard limit "auto-stops and marks the recording READY with a warning",
//      but §3's Recording has nowhere to put one — and `READY` on its own is
//      indistinguishable from a recording the user stopped deliberately, so
//      without a column the trace looks complete when it was cut short.
//      Ratified as D-042; an enum rather than free text because the renderer
//      and the M3-BE-06 compiler both branch on it.
//
//   5. `Recording.error String?`. §6/M3's acceptance criteria require that
//      stopping produces "a DRAFT skill within 60 s, or a FAILED recording
//      with a readable error", and §3's Recording has nowhere to put the
//      error. FAILED on its own does not distinguish a trace with a hole in
//      it from a compile the model refused, and the operator's only recourse
//      is to re-record something that may fail the same way. Free text, not
//      an enum, unlike `warning`: this carries a provider's verdict or a
//      schema violation, which is a sentence, not a vocabulary. Ratified as
//      D-044.
//
//   6. `Memory`, `MemoryTombstone`, and the three enums they need. §3 has no
//      memory at all, and D-069 put one in a file on the workspace volume —
//      which cannot express either scope the product requires (a user-global
//      memory a new bot starts with, and a per-bot memory), and which lives on
//      the one artifact §9 retention and D-056 both exist to reclaim.
//      WORKSPACE.md §1 already promised the opposite of D-069: "Bot memory
//      (Postgres rows) — durable across everything; it is not in the container
//      at all." This makes that line true. Same class of gap addition as
//      D-005's rules column and D-057's identities: nothing in §3 is renamed
//      or reinterpreted, two tables are added beside it. Ratified as D-073.
//
//   7. `MemoryDistillation`. CC-38's sweep needs a durable watermark — which
//      messages it has already read — and deriving one from existing rows does
//      not work: a pass that finds nothing worth remembering leaves no marker,
//      so the same window would be re-read and re-charged forever. One row per
//      bot, and deliberately NOT a column on `Bot`: this is sweep bookkeeping,
//      not part of what a bot IS, and §3's Bot is the table five people code
//      against. Ratified as D-078.
//
//   8. `User` gains `name`, `emailVerified`, `image` and `updatedAt`, `email`
//      becomes non-null, and `Session`, `Account` and `Verification` join it.
//      §3's User described the seeded singleton, which had no way in and so
//      needed neither an address nor a session. V1 gives every user three ways
//      in (Google, GitHub, a magic link), and every one of them reaches an
//      account BY EMAIL — a nullable email would mean a sign-in that cannot
//      find its own row. The four tables are Better Auth's core schema and are
//      GENERATED from `src/auth/better-auth.ts`, not written by hand: which
//      plugins that config enables decides which tables exist, and hand-adding
//      one is how the two drift into a runtime "column does not exist" that
//      reads like a missing migration. Ratified as D-083's prerequisite.
//
//   9. `ProviderConnection`, `ProviderModel`, `UserSettings` and the three
//      enums they need. §3 has no provider configuration at all, because D-002
//      put it in the environment: three keys, one process, one registry built
//      at boot. That stops being expressible the moment an install has two
//      users, since whichever key the process holds is one person's and every
//      turn bills it. Nothing in §3 is renamed; three tables are added beside
//      it, the same shape of gap addition as deviation 6. Ratified as D-082
//      and D-086.
//
//   10. `Skill.steps Json @default("[]")`. The compiler reads the trace and is
//      the one writer that knows what the skill does in plain language, but
//      §3's Skill stores only the machine halves (`description`, `script`) —
//      so the review surface had nothing to open on but the source, and
//      summarizing it again per view would re-ask a model for an answer one
//      compile already settled. One sentence per step, written at compile
//      time and carried forward unchanged across edits that do not restate
//      it (the same treatment `paramsSchema` gets). Empty for skills the bot
//      authored with `save_skill`, which had no trace to summarize. Ratified
//      as D-098.
//
//   11. `Subscription`, `UsageLedger`, `User.stripeCustomerId`, the admin
//      plugin's `User.role`/`banned`/`banReason`/`banExpires` and
//      `Session.impersonatedBy`, `Sandbox.meteredAt`, the computer-time
//      meter's watermark, and `ModelCharge` with the `CREWAID` provider kind
//      for the models a plan includes (D-157). §3 has no
//      billing because the PRD put it out of scope; the hosted service's plans
//      (D-148) need a subscription to read entitlements from and a per-period
//      record to check limits against. `Subscription` keeps
//      `@better-auth/stripe`'s column names but is written by the billing
//      module; the user columns are the Stripe and admin plugins', generated
//      like deviation 8's tables. Used
//      only on a cloud server with Stripe configured (D-152); elsewhere the
//      tables exist and stay empty. Ratified as D-156.
//
// Existing column names are contractual and guarded by schema-contract.test.ts.

generator client {
  provider = "prisma-client-js"
}

datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
}

/// V1-02 / deviation 7: the four Better Auth columns sit beside §3's three.
///
/// `name`, `emailVerified`, `image` and `updatedAt` are REQUIRED by Better
/// Auth's core schema, and `email` becomes non-null because an account is
/// reached by email under every one of the three sign-in methods — Google,
/// GitHub and the magic link. §3's nullable email described the seeded
/// singleton, which had no way in and therefore needed no address.
///
/// This block is GENERATED — `npx @better-auth/cli generate --config
/// src/ee/auth/better-auth-schema.config.ts`, which builds
/// src/auth/better-auth.ts with the cloud plugins too (D-238). Do not
/// hand-edit it, and re-run the CLI after touching either plugin list.
model User {
  gitCheckouts    GitCheckout[]
  gitPublications GitPublication[]
  gitRemoteWrites GitRemoteWrite[]
  id              String           @id @default(cuid())
  name            String
  email           String           @unique
  emailVerified   Boolean          @default(false)
  image           String?
  createdAt       DateTime         @default(now())
  updatedAt       DateTime         @updatedAt

  /// D-156: the Stripe customer, set at this user's first checkout. Declared
  /// by `@better-auth/stripe`, which finds a webhook's user by it.
  stripeCustomerId String?

  /// D-156: Better Auth's admin plugin. `role = 'admin'` is set by hand in the
  /// database, never through the API, and puts the account on the `internal`
  /// plan — no billing, no limits — for operating and testing the hosted
  /// service. The ban columns are the plugin's; nothing in Crewaid sets them.
  role       String?
  banned     Boolean?  @default(false)
  banReason  String?
  banExpires DateTime?

  sessions           Session[]
  accounts           Account[]
  telegramConnection TelegramConnection?
  telegramLinkTicket TelegramLinkTicket?
  telegramPendingBot TelegramPendingBot?

  onboarding            UserOnboarding?
  templateInstallations TemplateInstallation[]
  bots                  Bot[]
  mcpServers            McpServer[]
  attachments           Attachment[] // D-021: uploads owned from creation
  sandboxes             Sandbox[] // D-046: the user's computers, defaulting to one
  memories              Memory[] // D-073: what this user's bots know
  tombstones            MemoryTombstone[] // D-073: what they have been told to forget

  providerConnections ProviderConnection[] // D-082: this user's model providers
  settings            UserSettings? // D-086: the per-user `default` alias
  botGroups           BotGroup[] // D-131: sidebar groups of bots

  // D-133: Work — the user's projects, goals, tasks and what bots delivered.
  projects     Project[]
  goals        Goal[]
  tasks        Task[]
  taskSeries   TaskSeries[]
  taskEvents   TaskEvent[]
  deliverables Deliverable[]

  usageLedger  UsageLedger[] // D-156: what each billing period used
  modelCharges ModelCharge[] // D-157: each included-model call's cost
  voiceCharges VoiceCharge[] // D-223: each request's voice seconds
  voiceCalls   VoiceCall[] // D-222: each call's minutes

  // D-159 §3.14: run state carries its owner directly, so retention, export and
  // deletion never join through bots.
  runInterrupts  RunInterrupt[]
  toolDispatches ToolDispatch[]
  runEvents      RunEvent[]
}

/// D-233: a person's own Telegram bot, linked to the Telegram account that confirmed it.
/// The token is sealed (telegram-tokens.ts); the webhook secret is kept only as a digest.
model TelegramConnection {
  id                   String                       @id @default(cuid())
  userId               String                       @unique
  user                 User                         @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  telegramBotId        String                       @unique
  botUsername          String
  managed              Boolean                      @default(false)
  tokenCiphertext      String
  webhookSecretHash    String
  webhookUrl           String
  commandsDigest       String?
  telegramUserId       String
  chatId               String
  displayName          String
  selectedBotId        String?
  selectedAt           DateTime                     @default(now())
  lastMessageSequence  Int                          @default(0)
  lastRunSequence      Int                          @default(0)
  notifiedInterruptIds String[]                     @default([])
  createdAt            DateTime                     @default(now())
  updatedAt            DateTime                     @updatedAt
  attachmentDeliveries TelegramAttachmentDelivery[]
}

/// D-233: a bot waiting to be paired. A managed bot arrives with only its creator; a pasted
/// one with the account that pasted it. The right code fills the claim, and nothing is live
/// until that account confirms in Crewaid.
model TelegramPendingBot {
  telegramBotId       String    @id
  botUsername         String
  managed             Boolean
  ownerTelegramUserId String?
  userId              String?   @unique
  user                User?     @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  tokenCiphertext     String
  webhookSecretHash   String
  webhookUrl          String
  failedAttempts      Int       @default(0)
  claimedAt           DateTime?
  claimTelegramUserId String?
  claimChatId         String?
  claimDisplayName    String?
  expiresAt           DateTime
  createdAt           DateTime  @default(now())

  @@index([ownerTelegramUserId])
  @@index([expiresAt])
}

/// Token rotation emits manager updates, so retirement must outlive the deleted credentials.
model TelegramRetiredBot {
  telegramBotId String   @id
  retiredAt     DateTime @default(now())
}

model TelegramAttachmentDelivery {
  connectionId String
  connection   TelegramConnection @relation(fields: [connectionId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  attachmentId String
  attachment   Attachment         @relation(fields: [attachmentId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@id([connectionId, attachmentId])
}

/// D-233: the digest of a person's pairing code: one per account, single use, ten minutes.
model TelegramLinkTicket {
  tokenHash String   @id
  userId    String   @unique
  user      User     @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  expiresAt DateTime
}

/// An interactive sign-in. Revoking one ends interactive access at the next
/// frame (V1-09) — it does NOT stop already-authorized background work, which
/// carries its own persisted owner (V1-10).
///
/// GENERATED by the Better Auth CLI; see the note on `User`.
model Session {
  id        String   @id
  expiresAt DateTime
  token     String   @unique
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt
  ipAddress String?
  userAgent String?
  userId    String
  user      User     @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// D-156: the admin plugin's — the admin who opened this session as another user.
  impersonatedBy String?

  @@index([userId])
}

/// A linked identity provider proving WHO IS SIGNED IN.
///
/// ⚠️ NOT a model provider. D-082's `ProviderConnection` holds a key for
/// calling an inference API; the tokens here are Google's and GitHub's, and no
/// turn ever reads them. Two tables that both say "provider" is exactly the
/// confusion worth naming once, here, rather than discovering in a review.
///
/// `password` is present because Better Auth's core schema declares it and the
/// generator emits it; `emailAndPassword` is disabled in the config, so no row
/// ever carries one.
///
/// GENERATED by the Better Auth CLI; see the note on `User`.
model Account {
  id                    String    @id
  accountId             String
  providerId            String
  userId                String
  user                  User      @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  accessToken           String?
  refreshToken          String?
  idToken               String?
  accessTokenExpiresAt  DateTime?
  refreshTokenExpiresAt DateTime?
  scope                 String?
  password              String?
  createdAt             DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt             DateTime  @updatedAt

  @@index([userId])
}

/// One-shot verification values, which is where a magic link lives until it is
/// used. The row is consumed on use — that consumption is what makes a link
/// single-use, and V1-15 asserts the replay case against this table.
///
/// GENERATED by the Better Auth CLI; see the note on `User`.
model Verification {
  id         String   @id
  identifier String
  value      String
  expiresAt  DateTime
  createdAt  DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt  DateTime @updatedAt

  @@index([identifier])
}

/// D-156: one Stripe subscription, as Stripe last had it. Entitlements are
/// read from here.
///
/// Written only by the billing module's sync, which re-reads the subscription
/// from Stripe on every webhook event — never from an event's payload, because
/// Stripe delivers events in any order. `stripeSubscriptionId` is unique so two
/// deliveries racing for a new subscription make one row.
///
/// The column set is `@better-auth/stripe`'s subscription schema, kept so the
/// plugin's names stay recognisable, though its own subscription handling is
/// off and it no longer writes here. `referenceId` is the user's id and
/// `status` is Stripe's, verbatim; `plan` is `starter` or `pro`, and a Starter
/// subscription that is `trialing` is the trial.
model Subscription {
  id                   String    @id
  plan                 String
  referenceId          String
  stripeCustomerId     String?
  stripeSubscriptionId String?
  status               String    @default("incomplete")
  periodStart          DateTime?
  periodEnd            DateTime?
  trialStart           DateTime?
  trialEnd             DateTime?
  cancelAtPeriodEnd    Boolean?  @default(false)
  cancelAt             DateTime?
  canceledAt           DateTime?
  endedAt              DateTime?
  seats                Int?
  billingInterval      String?
  stripeScheduleId     String?

  @@unique([stripeSubscriptionId])
  @@index([referenceId])
}

model Bot {
  gitRemoteWrites     GitRemoteWrite[]
  gitCheckouts        GitCheckout[]
  gitPublications     GitPublication[]
  id                  String           @id @default(cuid())
  userId              String
  user                User             @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  kind                String           @default("STANDARD")
  name                String // "Quill"
  role                String // "Outbound Writer"
  avatarColor         String // hex, e.g. "#8B6F47"
  // Mascot silhouette, one of AvatarShapeSchema. NULLABLE on purpose: null
  // means "whichever shape the name hashes to", which is how every bot
  // created before this column looked. Defaulting it to a concrete shape
  // would have silently restyled every existing bot on deploy. It is written
  // only when an operator picks one.
  avatarShape         String?
  // Uploaded profile picture, preferred over the mascot when set. The upload
  // path does not exist yet (no storage, no route); the column is here so the
  // renderer's "url, else mascot" rule is written once against a real field
  // rather than retrofitted through the wire contract later.
  avatarUrl           String?
  systemPrompt        String           @db.Text
  modelId             String           @default("default") // key into the AI SDK provider registry
  status              BotStatus        @default(IDLE)
  sortOrder           Int              @default(0)
  pinned              Boolean          @default(false)
  hiddenFromSidebar   Boolean          @default(false)
  // M5 auto-review rules, ordered, first match wins; default `ask` (§6/M5).
  // Json rather than a table: always read as one whole list per bot, never
  // queried across bots. Shape lives in `@crewaid/shared` AutoReviewRuleSchema.
  autoReviewRules     Json             @default("[]")
  approvalMode        ApprovalMode     @default(AUTO) // D-144: Ask for approval, Approve for me, Full access
  // D-221: the ElevenLabs voice this bot speaks in. Null is the house voice.
  voiceId             String?
  pluginGrantRevision Int              @default(0)
  createdAt           DateTime         @default(now())
  updatedAt           DateTime         @updatedAt

  // D-046: the computer this bot works on. NULLABLE because a bot exists
  // before any machine does (D-041 provisions storage at creation, and a
  // never-started bot has none), and because detaching a bot must not delete
  // a machine its siblings are still using.
  sandboxId        String?
  sandbox          Sandbox?            @relation(fields: [sandboxId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  // D-131: the group this bot is in, if any. SetNull because deleting a group
  // moves its bots back to Unassigned; it never deletes a bot.
  groupId          String?
  group            BotGroup?           @relation(fields: [groupId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  conversation     Conversation?
  skills           Skill[]
  routines         Routine[]
  recordings       Recording[]
  turns            AgentTurn[]
  mcpServers       BotMcpServer[]
  identities       BotIdentity[]
  // Desired selection, never a claim that Chrome holds the profile.
  activeIdentityId String?
  activeIdentity   Identity?           @relation("SelectedIdentity", fields: [activeIdentityId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  identityRevision Int                 @default(0)
  memories         Memory[]            @relation("MemoryScope")
  /// Shared facts this bot established. Kept apart from the scope relation:
  /// a private memory's author IS its scope, but a workspace fact any bot
  /// wrote is read by every bot, and rendering it needs to name the bot whose
  /// work the claim describes.
  memoriesAuthored Memory[]            @relation("MemoryAuthor")
  distillation     MemoryDistillation?

  // D-133: Work. Deleting a bot unassigns its tasks and series (SetNull) and
  // deletes what it produced (D-136: deliverables cascade with the bot).
  assignedTasks  Task[]        @relation("TaskAssignee")
  createdTasks   Task[]        @relation("TaskCreator")
  assignedSeries TaskSeries[]  @relation("SeriesAssignee")
  taskEvents     TaskEvent[]   @relation("TaskEventActor")
  deliverables   Deliverable[]
  voiceCalls     VoiceCall[] // D-222

  @@index([sandboxId])
  @@index([groupId])
}

/// D-131: a named set of bots in the sidebar. What it shares with its members
/// is `instructions` (read into every member's prompt) and a folder on the
/// machine, `/home/bot/groups/<id>`. It is a label and a brief, never a role:
/// nothing about a group changes who a bot may message or what it may do.
model BotGroup {
  id           String   @id @default(cuid())
  userId       String
  user         User     @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  name         String
  instructions String   @default("") @db.Text
  sortOrder    Int      @default(0)
  collapsed    Boolean  @default(false)
  createdAt    DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt    DateTime @updatedAt

  bots Bot[]

  @@index([userId, sortOrder])
}

enum BotStatus {
  IDLE
  THINKING
  ACTING
  AWAITING_APPROVAL
  ERROR
}

model Conversation {
  id         String         @id @default(cuid())
  botId      String         @unique
  bot        Bot            @relation(fields: [botId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  createdAt  DateTime       @default(now())
  // D-131: the user has read every BOT message up to here. Null means never
  // marked, which counts every bot message as unread.
  lastReadAt DateTime?
  messages   Message[]
  runs       AgentTurn[]
  interrupts RunInterrupt[]
  dispatches ToolDispatch[]
  events     RunEvent[]
}

model Message {
  id             String        @id @default(cuid())
  conversationId String
  conversation   Conversation  @relation(fields: [conversationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  author         MessageAuthor
  fromBotId      String? // set when author = BOT
  content        String        @db.Text
  // AI_SDK threads: the SDK's parts, verbatim. TANSTACK threads: one
  // `ModelMessage` body, read through the one validating mapper (D-165).
  parts          Json?
  stopReason     String?
  // D-165: the thread's order. Taken when a message begins, so a user message
  // admitted while a reply streams sorts after the reply's earlier rows. An
  // Int, not a BigInt: rows reach JSON in many places and a BigInt cannot.
  sequence       Int           @unique @default(autoincrement())
  // The run that wrote this assistant or tool row; many rows per run.
  runId          String?
  run            AgentTurn?    @relation("RunMessages", fields: [runId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  chainId        String? // inter-bot conversation chain
  hopCount       Int           @default(0)
  // D-133: the task this message is about — a Start brief, or work another bot
  // delegated with `send_message({taskId})`. It is what files a deliverable the
  // turn produces (D-136 rule 2).
  taskId         String?
  task           Task?         @relation(fields: [taskId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  // D-136: the routine that posted this message; rule 3 files under its goal.
  routineId      String?
  routine        Routine?      @relation(fields: [routineId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  // D-147: a `/new` boundary. The turn's history seed reads only what comes
  // after the newest one, so an old subject stops riding along in every prompt
  // while the transcript keeps it. Deleting the row restores the context.
  startsTopic    Boolean       @default(false)
  createdAt      DateTime      @default(now())

  attachments  Attachment[]
  // D-073: what was learned here. SetNull on the far side — deleting a
  // transcript must not delete the fact it established.
  memories     Memory[]
  taskEvents   TaskEvent[]
  deliverables Deliverable[]

  @@index([conversationId, createdAt])
  @@index([conversationId, sequence])
  @@index([taskId])
  @@index([runId])
}

enum MessageAuthor {
  USER
  BOT
  SYSTEM
  TOOL
}

// The Attachment delete trigger also captures FK cascades in the same transaction.
model ArtifactDeletion {
  storageKey String   @id
  retryAt    DateTime @default(now())

  @@index([retryAt])
}

model Attachment {
  telegramDeliveries TelegramAttachmentDelivery[]
  id                 String                       @id @default(cuid())
  // An upload (POST /attachments) happens BEFORE the message it belongs to
  // exists, so messageId is nullable until a message claims the row; using a
  // ghost Message row instead would pollute conversation history.
  messageId          String?
  message            Message?                     @relation(fields: [messageId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  // D-021: ownership from CREATION, not from claiming. Without it, user B
  // can bind user A's unclaimed upload to B's message and download A's file
  // through the now-owned link — the D-010 IDOR class shipping under a green
  // test. No default: every row must have an owner from insert.
  ownerId            String
  user               User                         @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  filename           String
  mimeType           String
  sizeBytes          Int
  storageKey         String // key in the artifact store: R2, or ARTIFACT_DIR (D-220)
  // D-221: a recorded or spoken clip rather than a file. On a USER message it
  // marks the trigger as spoken, which is what makes the reply spoken too; an
  // audio file sent with Attach stays FILE and changes nothing.
  kind               AttachmentKind               @default(FILE)
  durationMs         Int?
  // D-020 addendum / J3 requires an upload-age for the 410 TTL fence and the
  // Timestamp for the unclaimed-attachment TTL fence and reaper.
  createdAt          DateTime                     @default(now())

  // D-136: the record that makes a bot's file findable. Absent for uploads.
  deliverable Deliverable?

  @@index([ownerId])
  @@index([createdAt]) // reaper scan: unclaimed AND older than TTL
}

// R9: one row per completed agent step, written before the next step starts.
model AgentTurn {
  sequence            Int            @unique @default(autoincrement())
  id                  String         @id @default(cuid())
  botId               String
  bot                 Bot            @relation(fields: [botId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  triggerMessageId    String
  // D-159 §3.5: the thread this run belongs to (`RunRecord.threadId`).
  conversationId      String
  conversation        Conversation   @relation(fields: [conversationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  status              TurnStatus     @default(running)
  stepIndex           Int            @default(0)
  workingMs           Int            @default(0)
  steps               Json           @default("[]") // array of completed AI SDK step results
  inputTokens         Int            @default(0)
  outputTokens        Int            @default(0)
  // D-009(3): cached reads bill at a fraction of fresh input once CC-11 lands.
  // Folding them into inputTokens makes CC-12's cost report and §7.2's hard
  // spend cap provably wrong — and §7.2 halts the product, so wrong numbers
  // mean either false halts or blown budgets.
  cachedInputTokens   Int            @default(0)
  cacheCreationTokens Int            @default(0)
  // D-178: validated canonical snapshot; counters above are materialized on this row.
  usage               Json?
  // CC-12: the resolved `provider:model` billing identity of the turn's
  // settled model call(s) — what /usage's cost table prices the counters
  // with. Nullable: turns settled before this column existed (or whose call
  // never settled) have no recorded identity and cost stays unknown for
  // them, never guessed. It is NOT the bot's current model: the composer's
  // model picker PATCHes the bot between sends, so a bot's history is
  // legitimately mixed-model.
  modelId             String?
  startedAt           DateTime       @default(now())
  endedAt             DateTime?
  stopReason          String?
  // D-165: why a `failed` run failed: a D-006 code, or WORKER_LOST for a run
  // whose worker died. Never a vendor's text.
  errorCode           String?
  // D-178: a safe sentence supplied by the server error projector, never vendor prose.
  errorMessage        String?
  // Nullable so completed/failed turns release the one-active-turn fence.
  // D-160: a TANSTACK run holds it only while `running`. An AI_SDK turn also
  // holds it while parked on an approval, until that path is deleted.
  activeKey           String?        @unique
  // D-159 §3.5: the library's fencing fields. Nullable, because "never set" and
  // false/0 mean different things to the run store.
  driverEpoch         Int?
  // D-177: one server producer claim, renewed against PostgreSQL's clock.
  workerId            String?
  leaseExpiresAt      DateTime?
  cancelRequested     Boolean?
  detachedSince       DateTime?
  sandboxKey          String?
  // D-160: the paused run whose interrupts this continuation answered. A turn is
  // a run and every continuation chained to it this way (D-164).
  resumesRunId        String?
  resumes             AgentTurn?     @relation("RunContinuation", fields: [resumesRunId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  continuations       AgentTurn[]    @relation("RunContinuation")
  // D-159 §3.7: set when the run's event log is closed; a tail stops here.
  eventsClosedAt      DateTime?
  messages            Message[]      @relation("RunMessages")
  interrupts          RunInterrupt[]
  dispatches          ToolDispatch[]
  events              RunEvent[]

  @@index([botId, startedAt])
  @@index([conversationId, startedAt])
  @@index([resumesRunId])
  @@index([status, leaseExpiresAt])
}

/// D-159 §3.6, D-165: a human-in-the-loop pause, durable across a restart.
/// Implements the library's `InterruptStore`. The id is minted by the server.
/// D-197: one row per call that can change something, reserved before it runs.
/// A second reservation of the same call finds this row and does not run it.
model ToolDispatch {
  id             String         @id @default(cuid())
  runId          String
  run            AgentTurn      @relation(fields: [runId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  conversationId String
  conversation   Conversation   @relation(fields: [conversationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  userId         String
  user           User           @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  toolCallId     String
  toolName       String
  effect         String
  argsDigest     String
  status         DispatchStatus @default(DISPATCHED)
  reference      String?
  dispatchedAt   DateTime       @default(now())
  settledAt      DateTime?

  @@unique([conversationId, toolCallId])
  @@index([runId])
  @@index([userId])
}

/// D-197: a dispatch is started, then known to have committed, known not to
/// have been sent, or not known either way.
enum DispatchStatus {
  DISPATCHED
  COMMITTED
  REFUSED
  UNCERTAIN
}

model RunInterrupt {
  id             String          @id @default(cuid())
  runId          String
  run            AgentTurn       @relation(fields: [runId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  conversationId String
  conversation   Conversation    @relation(fields: [conversationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  userId         String
  user           User            @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  status         InterruptStatus @default(pending)
  // Insertion order, which the store's list contract requires. Two interrupts
  // of one batch share a millisecond, so the timestamp cannot be the order.
  sequence       Int             @unique @default(autoincrement())
  requestedAt    DateTime        @default(now())
  resolvedAt     DateTime?
  // `crewaid.tool-approval`: the call, and its D-164 binding.
  payload        Json
  response       Json?

  // Protocol consumption follows policy acceptance at the successful boundary.
  // Both facets refer to this one proposal; a native record never grants approval.
  nativeInterruptId String?          @unique
  nativePayload     Json?
  nativeResponse    Json?
  nativeStatus      InterruptStatus?
  nativeRequestedAt DateTime?
  nativeResolvedAt  DateTime?

  @@index([conversationId, nativeStatus, nativeRequestedAt, sequence])
  @@index([runId, nativeStatus, nativeRequestedAt, sequence])
  @@index([conversationId, status, sequence])
  @@index([runId, status, sequence])
  @@index([userId])
}

enum InterruptStatus {
  pending
  resolved
  cancelled
}

/// D-159 §3.7, D-165: the delivery log. Every AG-UI event a run emits, in
/// order. `id` is the offset: monotonic, so it orders a run and a thread.
model RunEvent {
  id             BigInt       @id @default(autoincrement())
  runId          String
  run            AgentTurn    @relation(fields: [runId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  conversationId String
  conversation   Conversation @relation(fields: [conversationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  userId         String
  user           User         @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  // One AG-UI event, as the library emitted it.
  chunk          Json
  createdAt      DateTime     @default(now())

  @@index([runId, id])
  @@index([conversationId, id])
  @@index([userId])
}

/// D-159 §3.5: the library's `RunStatus`, so nothing translates on the way
/// through. `interrupted` waits for a person and is not terminal.
enum TurnStatus {
  running
  interrupted
  completed
  failed
  aborted
}

/// D-046: the computer. One machine, shared by the bots attached to it,
/// defaulting to one per user. The table keeps the name `Sandbox` — §0 rule 2
/// does not spend a rename across M2's lifecycle code, M4's capture, the §4.1
/// routes, the §4.3 frame header and the R7 allowlist on a word the UI already
/// calls "computer".
///
/// `id` is the workspace key: the volume is `crewaid-workspace-<id>` and carries
/// the label `crewaid.botId=<id>`. Both read as a bot id and no longer are. The
/// migration preserves them by giving each migrated machine the id of the bot
/// that used to own it, so not one durable volume is renamed or relabelled;
/// renaming the label would mean relabelling every operator's storage to gain
/// nothing a comment cannot say.
model Sandbox {
  id               String       @id @default(cuid())
  userId           String
  user             User         @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  sessionId        String? // harness session id, used for resumeSession
  containerId      String?
  state            SandboxState @default(STOPPED)
  // Only new Runtime workspaces may allocate a machine on their first admitted start.
  workspacePending Boolean      @default(false)
  // A failed or abandoned Runtime operation must confirm pause before releasing its slot.
  pausePending     Boolean      @default(false)
  widthPx          Int          @default(1280)
  heightPx         Int          @default(800)
  vncPort          Int?
  cdpPort          Int?
  bridgePort       Int?
  lastStartedAt    DateTime?
  lastError        String?      @db.Text
  // D-039 §10. Idle stop needs a "since when" that survives an orchestrator
  // restart; derived state would restart the clock on every deploy and make
  // idle stop unpredictable. `keepRunningUntil` is §8's explicit lease — one
  // with no expiry is a leak, and one held in memory does not survive the
  // restart it is most needed across.
  lastActiveAt     DateTime?
  keepRunningUntil DateTime?
  // D-156: how far this computer's running time has been counted against its
  // owner's plan. The meter counts from here (or from the last start, if
  // later) and moves it forward, so a restart neither loses nor repeats time.
  meteredAt        DateTime?

  bots       Bot[]
  identities Identity[]

  @@index([userId])
  @@index([state])
}

/// M5-BE-27 / D-057 — a named Chrome profile several bots may share.
///
/// Scoped to the COMPUTER rather than the user, because that is where the
/// thing actually is: an identity is a directory under one machine's
/// `--user-data-dir`, and the logged-in session inside it exists on that
/// machine's disk and nowhere else. A user with two computers who expects
/// "company-google" to work on both would otherwise be told it exists and
/// find it signed out, which is the failure this scoping makes impossible to
/// express.
///
/// `name` IS the profile directory, deliberately not a display name with a
/// slug beside it (`McpServer` has both because an operator types "Gmail" and
/// the wire needs "gmail"). Here the bot names it and the operator only reads
/// it back off an approval prompt, so a second column would be two truths
/// about one directory. `IdentityNameSchema` is what keeps it a legal one.
model Identity {
  id        String   @id @default(cuid())
  sandboxId String
  sandbox   Sandbox  @relation(fields: [sandboxId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  name      String
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  bots       BotIdentity[]
  selectedBy Bot[]         @relation("SelectedIdentity")

  @@unique([sandboxId, name])
}

/// M5-BE-27 / D-057 — the grant: this bot may open its seat on this identity.
///
/// Written when an operator approves the bot's `use_identity` call, and it
/// outlives the turn: the next seat this bot opens still goes to the granted
/// profile. That durability is the feature — a session stays reachable
/// without re-signing in — and it is also why the approval prompt names the
/// identity rather than just the tool.
model BotIdentity {
  botId      String
  bot        Bot      @relation(fields: [botId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  identityId String
  identity   Identity @relation(fields: [identityId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  grantedAt  DateTime @default(now())

  @@id([botId, identityId])
}

enum SandboxState {
  STOPPED
  STARTING
  RUNNING
  UNHEALTHY
  ERROR
}

model Recording {
  id           String            @id @default(cuid())
  botId        String
  bot          Bot               @relation(fields: [botId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  state        RecordingState    @default(RECORDING)
  intent       String?           @db.Text
  cdpAvailable Boolean           @default(true)
  // Deviation 4: why capture ended early, null when it ended when asked.
  warning      RecordingWarning?
  // Deviation 5: why a FAILED recording failed, null in every other state.
  error        String?           @db.Text
  startedAt    DateTime          @default(now())
  endedAt      DateTime?
  events       RecordingEvent[]
  // D-009: a list, not `Skill?`. Dropping @unique on Skill.sourceRecordingId
  // (so a skill can be re-versioned) turns this into one-to-many; Prisma
  // rejects a one-to-one whose foreign side is not unique.
  skills       Skill[]
}

enum RecordingState {
  RECORDING
  COMPILING
  READY
  FAILED
  DISCARDED
}

/// §6/M3's two hard limits, as the reason a recording stopped itself (M3-BE-05).
enum RecordingWarning {
  EVENT_LIMIT
  TIME_LIMIT
}

model RecordingEvent {
  id               String       @id @default(cuid())
  recordingId      String
  recording        Recording    @relation(fields: [recordingId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  tsOffsetMs       Int
  layer            CaptureLayer
  type             String // "click" | "type" | "navigate" | "input" | ...
  payload          Json
  screenshotKey    String? // set on X11 clicks only
  correlatedWithId String? // set by the M4 correlator

  @@index([recordingId, tsOffsetMs])
}

enum CaptureLayer {
  X11
  CDP
  ATSPI
}

model Skill {
  id                String      @id @default(cuid())
  botId             String? // null = available to all bots
  bot               Bot?        @relation(fields: [botId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  name              String // snake_case
  description       String      @db.Text
  paramsSchema      Json // JSON Schema for the skill's arguments
  // Deviation 10 (D-098): the compiler's plain-language summary of the task,
  // one sentence per step — what the review surface opens on instead of the
  // script. Empty for skills written by `save_skill`, which had no trace.
  steps             Json        @default("[]")
  script            String      @db.Text // TypeScript module source
  version           Int         @default(1)
  status            SkillStatus @default(DRAFT)
  // D-009: NOT @unique. M3 requires that editing a script bumps `version` and
  // keeps the previous version queryable, so v1 and v2 both point at the same
  // source recording — a unique constraint fails on the second edit.
  sourceRecordingId String?
  sourceRecording   Recording?  @relation(fields: [sourceRecordingId], references: [id])
  createdAt         DateTime    @default(now())
  runs              SkillRun[]

  @@unique([botId, name, version])
}

enum SkillStatus {
  DRAFT
  APPROVED
  ARCHIVED
}

model SkillRun {
  id        String    @id @default(cuid())
  skillId   String
  skill     Skill     @relation(fields: [skillId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  params    Json
  status    RunStatus @default(PENDING)
  logs      String?   @db.Text
  error     String?   @db.Text
  startedAt DateTime?
  endedAt   DateTime?
}

enum RunStatus {
  PENDING
  RUNNING
  SUCCEEDED
  FAILED
  CANCELLED
}

model McpServer {
  gitRemoteWrites GitRemoteWrite[]
  gitCheckouts    GitCheckout[]
  id              String           @id @default(cuid())
  userId          String
  user            User             @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  name            String // "Gmail"
  slug            String // "gmail" — used for tool namespacing
  category        String? // "Inbox And Collaboration"
  iconUrl         String?
  accessMode      PluginAccessMode @default(SELECTED)
  accessRevision  Int              @default(0)
  backend         McpBackend       @default(DIRECT)
  transport       McpTransport?    @default(HTTP)
  url             String?
  isPrivate       Boolean          @default(false) // user-added by URL vs directory entry
  authType        McpAuthType      @default(OAUTH2)
  status          McpStatus        @default(DISCONNECTED)
  lastError       String?          @db.Text
  createdAt       DateTime         @default(now())

  composioConnection ComposioConnection?
  credential         McpCredential?
  tools              McpTool[]
  bots               BotMcpServer[]
  oauthFlows         McpOAuthFlow[]

  @@unique([userId, slug])
}

enum McpTransport {
  HTTP
  SSE
}

enum McpAuthType {
  NONE
  OAUTH2
  HEADER
}

enum McpStatus {
  DISCONNECTED
  CONNECTING
  CONNECTED
  NEEDS_REAUTH
  ERROR
}

// Both token columns are encrypted at rest with CREDENTIAL_KEY before they
// reach Prisma (M6-BE-04, apps/orchestrator/src/mcp/mcp-credentials.ts — the
// only writer and the only reader). Never log, never return over the API,
// never pass to a sandbox.
//
// `authMetadata` is the exception, and it is not a credential: it holds the
// three coordinates a REFRESH needs and only the connect flow ever knew —
// `{ tokenEndpoint, clientId, resource }`. A refresh token alone cannot mint
// a new access token without them, and re-discovery cannot supply them for a
// private server that answers 401 without a bearer (D-103). Nothing in it is
// secret: the client id belongs to a public PKCE client this orchestrator
// registered with no secret, the token endpoint is a URL the user's browser
// already visited, and `resource` is the server's own URL. Nullable, because
// rows written before M6-BE-05 carry none — a credential with no metadata is
// usable until it expires and then needs a reconnect.
model McpCredential {
  id           String    @id @default(cuid())
  serverId     String    @unique
  server       McpServer @relation(fields: [serverId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  accessToken  String    @db.Text
  refreshToken String?   @db.Text
  expiresAt    DateTime?
  scopes       String[]
  authMetadata Json?
  revision     Int       @default(0)
  updatedAt    DateTime  @updatedAt
}

// One browser trip in flight, from `POST /mcp/servers/:id/connect` until the
// redirect lands on `GET /mcp/oauth/callback` (M6-BE-03). A row rather than
// process memory because the callback arrives minutes later, from a browser,
// with no session — and because a restart in between must not strand the
// server in CONNECTING.
//
// `state` is unique because it is the whole lookup: it is the only thing the
// redirect carries that names this row, and `codeVerifier` is what makes the
// code useless to anyone else who sees it. Both are single-use — the callback
// deletes the row it finds. `tokenEndpoint`, `clientId` and `redirectUri` are
// recorded at connect time so the exchange uses the endpoint the authorize
// URL was built against, rather than re-discovering it minutes later.
//
// ⚠️ NOTE WHAT IS NOT HERE: the server's `status` is left alone for the life
// of a flow. `CONNECTING` is a fact about this row, not about the credential —
// see `toSummary` in mcp-servers.service.ts.
model McpOAuthFlow {
  id            String    @id @default(cuid())
  state         String    @unique
  serverId      String
  server        McpServer @relation(fields: [serverId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  codeVerifier  String
  clientId      String
  redirectUri   String
  tokenEndpoint String
  resource      String
  scopes        String[]
  createdAt     DateTime  @default(now())
  expiresAt     DateTime

  @@index([serverId])
}

enum McpToolSource {
  REMOTE
  CREWAID_GIT
}

model McpTool {
  source      McpToolSource @default(REMOTE)
  id          String        @id @default(cuid())
  serverId    String
  server      McpServer     @relation(fields: [serverId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  name        String // as reported by the server
  description String        @db.Text
  inputSchema Json
  readOnly    Boolean       @default(false) // classified at list time, see M6
  refreshedAt DateTime      @default(now())

  @@unique([serverId, name])
}

model BotMcpServer {
  botId    String
  bot      Bot       @relation(fields: [botId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  serverId String
  server   McpServer @relation(fields: [serverId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  enabled  Boolean   @default(true)

  @@id([botId, serverId])
}

model Routine {
  id          String        @id @default(cuid())
  botId       String
  bot         Bot           @relation(fields: [botId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  name        String
  cron        String // 5-field cron
  timezone    String        @default("America/Port-au-Prince")
  endsOn      String?       @db.VarChar(10) // inclusive date in the routine's timezone
  prompt      String?       @db.Text // exactly one of prompt or skillId
  skillId     String?
  skillParams Json?
  enabled     Boolean       @default(true)
  lastRunAt   DateTime?
  lastStatus  RunStatus?
  /// D-125 (M8-BE-08): who created it. A bot may schedule its own follow-ups,
  /// and the operator must be able to tell those apart from their own — the
  /// two are not equally trusted. Set once at creation; nothing updates it.
  origin      RoutineOrigin @default(OPERATOR)
  /// D-133: the goal this routine works toward. SetNull: deleting a goal
  /// unlinks its routines and stops none of them.
  goalId      String?
  goal        Goal?         @relation(fields: [goalId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  messages    Message[]

  @@index([goalId])
}

/// D-125 — the provenance of a `Routine`.
enum RoutineOrigin {
  OPERATOR
  BOT
}

/// D-073 — what a bot knows that is not in its transcript.
///
/// Scope is two nullable columns rather than an enum, because that is what
/// makes the query a filter instead of a branch: `userId = $1 AND (botId IS
/// NULL OR botId = $2)` returns the global memory and this bot's own in one
/// pass. A `projectId` lands beside them the first time a user can have two
/// checkouts; today one machine per user (D-046) means every bot works on the
/// same one, so a project scope would discriminate between cases the product
/// cannot yet express.
model Memory {
  id          String  @id @default(cuid())
  userId      String
  user        User    @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  /// NULL = every bot this user has. Set = this bot's own. Cascade rather than
  /// SetNull: SetNull would PROMOTE a deleted bot's private notes to the
  /// global memory, which is the one wrong answer available here.
  botId       String?
  bot         Bot?    @relation("MemoryScope", fields: [botId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  /// The bot that ESTABLISHED a shared fact — null when nobody wrote it in a
  /// bot's voice. Not the scope: a workspace fact may be authored by any bot
  /// on the team, and the author's name is what stops a teammate reading "the
  /// Kreova report lives at …" as its own résumé. SetNull, not Cascade — a
  /// deleted teammate's facts survive as unattributed rather than vanishing
  /// with it.
  authorBotId String?
  authorBot   Bot?    @relation("MemoryAuthor", fields: [authorBotId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  kind   MemoryKind
  source MemorySource
  status MemoryStatus @default(ACTIVE)
  /// One self-contained claim. Bounded at write time, not here: a CHECK
  /// constraint has no Prisma spelling and would read as permanent drift.
  text   String       @db.Text
  /// A DECISION's rationale, or the attributed origin of an OBSERVED claim.
  /// Never injected as fact — see D-079.
  detail String?      @db.Text

  /// The message that established it, when there was one. SetNull, not
  /// Cascade: deleting a transcript must not delete what was learned from it.
  messageId String?
  message   Message? @relation(fields: [messageId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  /// Where an OBSERVED claim came from when it was not a message — a URL, a
  /// file path. Free text, because it carries a location rather than a
  /// vocabulary.
  sourceRef String?

  /// A correction points the old row at the new one. Nothing is deleted, so a
  /// superseded fact stays searchable as its own audit trail.
  supersededById String?
  supersededBy   Memory?   @relation("MemorySupersession", fields: [supersededById], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  supersedes     Memory[]  @relation("MemorySupersession")
  supersededAt   DateTime?
  /// TASK_STATE ages out of the prompt. Durable kinds leave this null.
  expiresAt      DateTime?

  /// Raised when a turn re-establishes something already held. A ranking
  /// input, never the FIRST one: a model inference confirmed repeatedly must
  /// not outrank something the user stated once (D-074).
  confirmations Int       @default(1)
  lastUsedAt    DateTime?
  useCount      Int       @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  /// The turn-start read: this user, this bot or global, still live.
  @@index([userId, botId, status])
  @@index([userId, updatedAt])
}

/// D-073 — a claim the user erased, kept so it cannot come back.
///
/// Without this the memory system has a live defect rather than a gap: the
/// extraction sweep (CC-38) reads transcripts, so a fact removed by `forget`
/// is re-derived on the next pass from the very conversation that established
/// it, and the user's correction silently undoes itself. The hash is of the
/// normalised claim, so a reworded restatement of the same thing is caught
/// too.
model MemoryTombstone {
  id        String   @id @default(cuid())
  userId    String
  user      User     @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  /// Scoped the same way the memory was, so erasing a bot's private note does
  /// not also suppress the user-global fact it happened to resemble.
  botId     String?
  claimHash String
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  /// Lookup only. The UNIQUENESS is a COALESCE expression index in the
  /// migration, and it has to be, because Postgres treats NULLs as DISTINCT
  /// in a unique index: `@@unique([userId, botId, claimHash])` would enforce
  /// nothing at all for the user-global rows, which are the common case. A
  /// restarted extraction sweep would then insert a second tombstone for the
  /// same erased claim, and the idempotency this table exists to provide
  /// would be a no-op exactly where it matters most.
  @@index([userId, botId, claimHash])
}

/// What a memory IS, which is what decides how it decays and whether it may be
/// injected at all. TASK_STATE replaces rather than accumulates; RESOURCE
/// points at a source rather than replacing it.
enum MemoryKind {
  PREFERENCE
  CONVENTION
  FACT
  DECISION
  TASK_STATE
  RESOURCE
}

/// Where a claim came from, in descending authority. The order matters: D-074
/// resolves conflicts by source before recency, so a DISTILLED guess never
/// silently overrides a TOLD statement.
enum MemorySource {
  TOLD
  DISTILLED
  OBSERVED
  IMPORTED
}

/// Whether a claim counts yet. CANDIDATE is what makes automatic extraction
/// safe to run: it is searchable but never injected, so a wrong inference is a
/// search result rather than a standing truth.
enum MemoryStatus {
  CANDIDATE
  ACTIVE
  SUPERSEDED
  FORGOTTEN
}

/// D-078 — how far CC-38's extraction sweep has read.
///
/// Advanced only when a pass COMMITS, so a crash re-reads a window rather than
/// skipping it: re-reading costs one model call and is deduplicated by claim
/// hash, while skipping loses the only chance to notice something.
model MemoryDistillation {
  botId String @id
  bot   Bot    @relation(fields: [botId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// The newest message this bot has been distilled through. Paired with its
  /// timestamp because ids are not ordered — cuid sorts lexically, not
  /// chronologically, so the id alone cannot answer "what is newer than this".
  throughMessageId String?
  throughCreatedAt DateTime?

  lastRunAt         DateTime @default(now())
  /// For the evidence pack: how much a sweep is actually producing.
  candidatesWritten Int      @default(0)
}

// ── Provider connections (D-082 … D-086) ─────────────────────────────────────

/// A model provider this user can call, and the credential to call it with.
///
/// D-082: the ROW is the identity. `name` is what an operator called it and is
/// display only — two connections may both be called "work", and the first
/// thing a name-keyed lookup does is silently pick one of them.
///
/// D-083: `credentialCiphertext` is AES-256-GCM output produced by the vault,
/// never a value any route accepts or returns. The owner id and this row's id
/// are bound in as additional authenticated data, so a ciphertext moved
/// between rows fails to decrypt rather than decrypting into the wrong
/// account. `credentialKeyVersion` names the keyring entry that produced it,
/// which is what makes rotation possible without re-encrypting everything in
/// one transaction.
model ProviderConnection {
  id     String @id @default(cuid())
  userId String
  user   User   @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  kind     ProviderKind
  name     String
  /// Set for CUSTOM only. The built-ins have fixed endpoints the SDK owns, and
  /// a nullable column is what keeps "which endpoint" from becoming a question
  /// anyone asks about an OpenAI connection.
  endpoint String?

  authKind ConnectionAuthKind @default(API_KEY)
  state    ConnectionState    @default(NEEDS_CREDENTIAL)

  /// ⚠️ CIPHERTEXT. Never selected into a response, never logged, never sent
  /// to a sandbox. The only reader is the owner-scoped credential service.
  credentialCiphertext String?   @db.Text
  credentialKeyVersion Int?
  credentialSetAt      DateTime?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  models ProviderModel[]

  /// ⚠️ THE UNIQUENESS IS NOT HERE. One connection per BUILT-IN kind is a
  /// PARTIAL unique index — `("userId", "kind") WHERE kind <> 'CUSTOM'` —
  /// written by hand in
  /// `migrations/20260916230000_v1_accounts_and_provider_connections`.
  ///
  /// Prisma cannot express a partial index, and the closest thing it can,
  /// `@@unique([userId, kind])`, would also cap CUSTOM connections at one per
  /// user, which is the opposite of the requirement (D-082). Enforcing it in a
  /// service instead is enforcement only on the paths that call that service,
  /// and the second call site is always written by someone who did not know
  /// about the first.
  @@index([userId])
}

/// A model a connection can serve.
///
/// D-082 keeps three identities apart: `id` is what a `Bot.modelId` stores,
/// `upstreamModelId` is what goes on the wire to the provider, and `label` is
/// what a person reads. Collapsing any two means a rename repoints bots.
model ProviderModel {
  id           String             @id @default(cuid())
  connectionId String
  connection   ProviderConnection @relation(fields: [connectionId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  upstreamModelId String
  label           String
  contextWindow   Int

  /// D-087: what the MODEL can do. Says nothing about what an execution
  /// backend can do with it — a future runtime may refuse tools this model
  /// supports, and that is a separate question with a separate answer.
  supportsTools     Boolean @default(true)
  supportsVision    Boolean @default(false)
  supportsReasoning Boolean @default(false)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  /// D-086: the users whose `default` alias resolves here.
  defaultFor UserSettings[]

  /// One row per upstream id per connection. Two rows for `gpt-5.1` on one
  /// connection are two ids for one model, and a bot pointed at the deleted
  /// one fails resolution while the picker still shows the model as present.
  @@unique([connectionId, upstreamModelId])
  @@index([connectionId])
}

/// Per-user settings. One row per user, created lazily on first write.
///
/// D-086: `defaultModelId` is what the literal `default` in `Bot.modelId`
/// resolves to, replacing the `DEFAULT_MODEL_ID` environment variable. Null
/// means the user has not chosen one, and a bot on the alias then fails
/// resolution with a typed error the settings pane can act on — rather than
/// silently borrowing whichever model the process was configured with, which
/// on a two-user install is somebody else's provider account.
///
/// `onDelete: Restrict` is the referential half of V1-05: deleting a model a
/// default still points at is refused until the reference moves. A connection
/// is covered transitively — its models cascade, and the first one a default
/// points at refuses the cascade.
model UserSettings {
  userId String @id
  user   User   @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// The model only. The connection is reached through it, in the same
  /// `include`, rather than being denormalised alongside — a second column
  /// naming the connection can disagree with the one the model actually
  /// belongs to, and the resolution path would then have two answers and no
  /// rule for which wins.
  defaultModelId String?
  defaultModel   ProviderModel? @relation(fields: [defaultModelId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// D-234: the language and timezone the person chose, null for "Follow
  /// System"/"Auto-detect", beside what a signed-in desktop last reported for
  /// its computer. Two columns each because one cannot tell a choice from a
  /// detection, and a second computer must update the detection without
  /// overwriting the choice. Values are `LocaleSchema` codes and IANA zones,
  /// validated at the edge.
  language       String?
  timezone       String?
  systemLanguage String?
  systemTimezone String?

  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  @@index([defaultModelId])
}

/// What a connection talks to. CUSTOM is any public HTTPS endpoint speaking
/// the OpenAI Chat Completions shape (D-085 constrains which hosts).
enum ProviderKind {
  OPENAI
  ANTHROPIC
  OPENROUTER
  CUSTOM
  /// D-157: the models a hosted plan includes; made by the server, no key.
  CREWAID
  /// D-221: a speech key, not a model provider. It holds no models and never
  /// serves a turn; voice reads its credential through the same vault.
  ELEVENLABS
}

/// D-221: what an attachment is for.
enum AttachmentKind {
  FILE
  VOICE_NOTE
}

/// D-084: V1 accepts one kind. It is an enum rather than an inferred boolean
/// so a V2 subscription-backed kind arrives as a migration and a contract
/// change a client can refuse, instead of as a nullable column nobody reads.
enum ConnectionAuthKind {
  API_KEY
}

/// Whether a connection can currently serve a turn. NEEDS_CREDENTIAL is the
/// state a connection is BORN in and the one it returns to when its key is
/// removed, which is why it is stored rather than derived.
enum ConnectionState {
  ACTIVE
  NEEDS_CREDENTIAL
  DISABLED
}

// ── Work (D-132 … D-140, docs/PRD-WORK.md §4) ───────────────────────────────
//
// A record the user and their bots read and write, never a workflow: nothing
// here is read by a scheduler or a trigger, except TaskSeries, which puts an
// occurrence on the board when the user's schedule says so (D-137).
//
// A project is referenced with NoAction rather than Restrict. Both refuse to
// delete a project something still points at — the service answers 409 before
// either is reached — but NoAction is checked at the END of the statement, so
// deleting a User can cascade to its projects and its tasks in one statement
// in whichever order Postgres chooses.

/// D-133: a grouping of goals and tasks. Not a folder, and not D-073's
/// reserved memory project scope.
model Project {
  id          String        @id @default(cuid())
  userId      String
  user        User          @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  name        String
  description String?       @db.Text
  color       String // hex, same vocabulary as Bot.avatarColor
  status      ProjectStatus @default(ACTIVE)
  sortOrder   Int           @default(0)
  createdAt   DateTime      @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime      @updatedAt

  goals        Goal[]
  tasks        Task[]
  series       TaskSeries[]
  deliverables Deliverable[]

  @@index([userId, status])
}

enum ProjectStatus {
  ACTIVE
  ARCHIVED
}

/// D-133: an outcome with a target date. The project is optional.
model Goal {
  id        String     @id @default(cuid())
  userId    String
  user      User       @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  projectId String?
  project   Project?   @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id], onDelete: NoAction)
  title     String
  outcome   String?    @db.Text
  targetOn  String?    @db.VarChar(10) // calendar date, same shape as Routine.endsOn
  status    GoalStatus @default(ACTIVE)
  closedAt  DateTime?
  createdAt DateTime   @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime   @updatedAt

  tasks        Task[]
  series       TaskSeries[]
  routines     Routine[]
  deliverables Deliverable[]

  @@index([userId, status])
}

enum GoalStatus {
  ACTIVE
  ACHIEVED
  ABANDONED
}

model Task {
  id             String      @id @default(cuid())
  userId         String
  user           User        @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  projectId      String? // null = Inbox
  project        Project?    @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id], onDelete: NoAction)
  goalId         String?
  goal           Goal?       @relation(fields: [goalId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  parentId       String? // one level: a parent's parentId is always null (service-enforced)
  parent         Task?       @relation("Subtasks", fields: [parentId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  subtasks       Task[]      @relation("Subtasks")
  title          String
  brief          String?     @db.Text
  status         TaskStatus  @default(TODO)
  blockedReason  String?     @db.Text
  assigneeBotId  String?
  assigneeBot    Bot?        @relation("TaskAssignee", fields: [assigneeBotId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  origin         TaskOrigin  @default(OPERATOR) // who created it; mirrors Routine.origin (D-125)
  seriesId       String? // set on an occurrence; SetNull if the series is deleted
  series         TaskSeries? @relation(fields: [seriesId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  occursAt       DateTime? // scheduled instant: retry identity, including hourly/minute repeats
  occursOn       String?     @db.VarChar(10) // local date for display and date-range queries
  createdByBotId String?
  createdByBot   Bot?        @relation("TaskCreator", fields: [createdByBotId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  dueOn          String?     @db.VarChar(10)
  rank           String // lexicographic order within a column; a drag is one row write
  startedAt      DateTime?
  completedAt    DateTime?
  createdAt      DateTime    @default(now())
  updatedAt      DateTime    @updatedAt

  events       TaskEvent[]
  deliverables Deliverable[]
  messages     Message[]

  @@unique([seriesId, occursAt]) // one occurrence per scheduled firing, however often retried
  @@index([userId, status])
  @@index([assigneeBotId, status])
  @@index([parentId])
  @@index([goalId])
  @@index([projectId])
  @@index([userId, goalId, occursOn])
}

enum TaskStatus {
  TODO
  IN_PROGRESS
  BLOCKED
  IN_REVIEW
  DONE
  CANCELLED
}

/// D-137: a task that comes back. Each firing creates one Task with seriesId set.
model TaskSeries {
  id            String     @id @default(cuid())
  userId        String
  user          User       @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  title         String
  brief         String?    @db.Text
  assigneeBotId String?
  assigneeBot   Bot?       @relation("SeriesAssignee", fields: [assigneeBotId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  projectId     String?
  project       Project?   @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id], onDelete: NoAction)
  goalId        String?
  goal          Goal?      @relation(fields: [goalId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  cron          String // same CronSchema/TimezoneSchema as Routine
  timezone      String
  endsOn        String?    @db.VarChar(10)
  enabled       Boolean    @default(true)
  lastFiredAt   DateTime?
  origin        TaskOrigin @default(OPERATOR)
  createdAt     DateTime   @default(now())
  updatedAt     DateTime   @updatedAt

  occurrences Task[]

  @@index([userId, enabled])
  @@index([goalId])
}

enum TaskOrigin {
  OPERATOR
  BOT
}

/// "Who did what, when." Append-only. The payload's shape per type lives in
/// `TaskEventPayloadSchemas` in @crewaid/shared.
model TaskEvent {
  id         String        @id @default(cuid())
  taskId     String
  task       Task          @relation(fields: [taskId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  userId     String
  user       User          @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  actor      TaskActor
  actorBotId String? // SetNull: history outlives the bot
  actorBot   Bot?          @relation("TaskEventActor", fields: [actorBotId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  actorLabel String // name at the time, so a deleted bot still reads "Backend"
  type       TaskEventType
  payload    Json
  startKey   String? // STARTED only; identifies retries even after status changes (D-135)
  messageId  String? // BOT reply for a tool edit, Start brief for STARTED; null for board edits
  message    Message?      @relation(fields: [messageId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  createdAt  DateTime      @default(now())

  @@unique([userId, startKey])
  @@index([taskId, createdAt])
}

enum TaskActor {
  USER
  BOT
}

enum TaskEventType {
  CREATED
  EDITED
  ASSIGNED
  UNASSIGNED
  STARTED
  STATUS_CHANGED
  NOTE
  DELIVERABLE_LINKED
}

/// D-136: something a bot produced that the user may want to find again.
model Deliverable {
  id           String          @id @default(cuid())
  userId       String
  user         User            @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  kind         DeliverableKind
  title        String
  summary      String?         @db.Text
  botId        String? // producer. Cascade: a deleted bot takes its deliverables
  bot          Bot?            @relation(fields: [botId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  attachmentId String?         @unique // Cascade: the record goes with its file
  attachment   Attachment?     @relation(fields: [attachmentId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  // D-147: SetNull, not Cascade. A deliverable is a record of work, and
  // clearing or pruning a conversation must not erase what the bot produced.
  messageId    String?
  message      Message?        @relation(fields: [messageId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  url          String?
  sourcePath   String? // where in the workspace the file was attached from
  taskId       String?
  task         Task?           @relation(fields: [taskId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  goalId       String?
  goal         Goal?           @relation(fields: [goalId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  projectId    String? // denormalised from task/goal (D-136)
  project      Project?        @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  searchText   String?         @db.Text // P1: bounded extract of a text-like file
  createdAt    DateTime        @default(now())

  @@index([userId, createdAt])
  @@index([taskId])
  @@index([goalId])
  @@index([projectId])
  @@index([botId])
  @@index([messageId])
}

enum DeliverableKind {
  FILE
  LINK
  MESSAGE
}

/// D-144 — how a bot's actions are approved, chosen from the composer.
enum ApprovalMode {
  ASK
  AUTO
  FULL
}

/// D-156: what one user used in one billing period, which is what a plan's
/// limits are checked against.
///
/// Keyed by the period's start, so a new period is a new row and usage resets
/// without anything having to zero it. A trial's period is the trial itself.
/// Money is micro-dollars in a BigInt: summing per-call costs as floats drifts.
model UsageLedger {
  id             String   @id @default(cuid())
  userId         String
  user           User     @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  periodStart    DateTime
  periodEnd      DateTime
  computeSeconds Int      @default(0)
  modelMicroUsd  BigInt   @default(0)
  /// D-223: seconds of audio heard (dictation, voice messages) on Crewaid's key.
  listenSeconds  Int      @default(0)
  /// D-223: seconds of audio made (spoken replies) on Crewaid's key.
  speakSeconds   Int      @default(0)
  createdAt      DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt      DateTime @updatedAt

  @@unique([userId, periodStart])
}

/// D-157: one call to an included model, and what it cost. `UsageLedger` holds
/// the period's total that the allowance is checked against; these rows are
/// the itemisation behind it, so "where did my allowance go" has an answer.
///
/// Written BEFORE the call as a reservation — an estimate already counted in
/// the total, which is what makes concurrent calls see each other — and
/// settled after it to the actual cost: OpenRouter's own figure, or for a
/// cancelled or failed call an estimate of what it used. `settledAt` null is a
/// reservation still standing; if settling never succeeds it stays counted at
/// the estimate, so a charge is never lost.
model ModelCharge {
  id              String    @id @default(cuid())
  userId          String
  user            User      @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  periodStart     DateTime
  upstreamModelId String
  costMicroUsd    BigInt
  settledAt       DateTime?
  createdAt       DateTime  @default(now())

  @@index([userId, createdAt])
}

/// D-223: which voice allowance a request draws on.
enum VoiceUsageKind {
  LISTEN
  SPEAK
}

/// D-223: one request on Crewaid's ElevenLabs key, and the seconds it took
/// from the period. Reserved at an estimate before the request, settled to
/// the audio's real length after it, like D-157's `ModelCharge`.
model VoiceCharge {
  id          String         @id @default(cuid())
  userId      String
  user        User           @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  periodStart DateTime
  kind        VoiceUsageKind
  seconds     Int
  settledAt   DateTime?
  createdAt   DateTime       @default(now())

  @@index([userId, createdAt])
}

/// D-222: one live call with a bot, and the minutes it took from the period.
/// `durationSeconds` is ElevenLabs' own count, read once the call ends; until
/// then the call counts as its elapsed time, capped at `reservedSeconds`.
model VoiceCall {
  id              String    @id @default(cuid())
  userId          String
  user            User      @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  botId           String
  bot             Bot       @relation(fields: [botId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  /// ElevenLabs' conversation id, once the call connects.
  conversationId  String?   @unique
  periodStart     DateTime
  /// What was left of the period's minutes when the call started.
  reservedSeconds Int
  startedAt       DateTime  @default(now())
  endedAt         DateTime?
  durationSeconds Int?
  settledAt       DateTime?

  @@index([userId, periodStart])
  @@index([botId])
}

// Composio owns OAuth tokens. These coordinates bind one installation to one account.
enum McpBackend {
  DIRECT
  COMPOSIO
}

model ComposioConnection {
  serverId         String    @id
  server           McpServer @relation(fields: [serverId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  toolkit          String
  authConfigId     String
  accountId        String?
  pendingAccountId String?
  pendingExpiresAt DateTime?
  sessionId        String?
  revision         Int       @default(0)
  removing         Boolean   @default(false)
  cleanupAccounts  String[]  @default([])
  cleanupSessions  String[]  @default([])
}

enum PluginAccessMode {
  SELECTED
  ALL
}

enum GitCheckoutStatus {
  CREATING
  READY
  FAILED
}

enum GitPublicationStatus {
  PREPARED
  SENDING
  CONFIRMED
  UNCERTAIN
}

model GitCheckout {
  id                String            @id @default(cuid())
  userId            String
  user              User              @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  botId             String
  bot               Bot               @relation(fields: [botId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  serverId          String
  server            McpServer         @relation(fields: [serverId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  repository        String
  repositoryId      String
  defaultBranch     String
  path              String
  status            GitCheckoutStatus @default(CREATING)
  activeOperationId String?
  activeUntil       DateTime?
  createdAt         DateTime          @default(now())
  publications      GitPublication[]

  @@unique([botId, path])
  @@index([userId, serverId])
}

model GitPublication {
  id                 String               @id @default(cuid())
  userId             String
  user               User                 @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  botId              String
  bot                Bot                  @relation(fields: [botId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  checkoutId         String
  checkout           GitCheckout          @relation(fields: [checkoutId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  targetRepository   String
  targetRepositoryId String
  branch             String
  intendedHead       String
  tree               String
  previousHead       String?
  confirmedHead      String?
  status             GitPublicationStatus @default(PREPARED)
  updatedAt          DateTime             @updatedAt

  @@unique([checkoutId, targetRepositoryId, branch])
  @@index([userId, botId])
}

/// D-208: recovery receipt for the existing GitHub fork and PR tools.
model GitRemoteWrite {
  id        String               @id @default(cuid())
  userId    String
  user      User                 @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  botId     String
  bot       Bot                  @relation(fields: [botId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  serverId  String
  server    McpServer            @relation(fields: [serverId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  toolName  String
  argsHash  String
  input     Json
  accountId String
  status    GitPublicationStatus @default(PREPARED)
  reference String?
  updatedAt DateTime             @updatedAt

  @@unique([botId, serverId, toolName, argsHash])
  @@index([userId, botId])
}

/// D-229: account experience progress survives chat clearing.
model UserOnboarding {
  userId String @id
  user User @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  welcomeSeen Boolean @default(false)
  discovery String @default("NEW")
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt
}

/// D-229: committed setup receipt, also the retry fence.
model TemplateInstallation {
  id String @id @default(cuid())
  userId String
  user User @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  idempotencyKey String
  templateId String
  revision String
  configuration String @db.Text
  groupId String?
  botIds String[]
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  @@unique([userId, idempotencyKey])
  @@index([userId, createdAt])
}
